- New customer-data resource = full vertical (BE-0136 pattern, project_grants migration 0014, store/project_grant.go, httpapi/project_grants.go): one story is allowed to land the whole vertical when the resource has no foundation yet (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database (composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger), CHECKs the enum-shaped columns to a closed set (principal_kind IN ('usr','sa'), role IN (six built-in roles)), and uses a UNIQUE index with COALESCE(env_id,'') / COALESCE(svc_id,'') so per-principal scope-tuple dedup works through NULLs (Postgres unique constraints treat NULLs as distinct by default until v15's NULLS NOT DISTINCT). The repository (ProjectGrantRepository) and reader adapter (ProjectGrantReader) follow OrganizationVariableReader exactly — repo accepts a Querier for reads, mutations need *Tx, reader composes the repo through Store.Read; cross-tenant queries return empty (no rows) at the SQL leg, but the LIST-FOR-PARENT shape (list grants OF a project) requires the reader to Get the project first inside the same transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound — never as an empty list, which would invite an agent to believe the project exists with no grants. The HTTP layer adds a new ProjectGrantReader port + handler + projection (projectGrantResource), and the route plugs into the existing projectIDResolver and policy.ActionProjectGrantsRead in routes.go's newRouteTable. EnvironmentID / ServiceID stay nullable pointers all the way from the schema to the wire so the (project | environment | service)-scoped distinction reaches an agent intact; the principal projects as a nested object Principal(id, kind) for the same reason the